<h2 class="doc-h2">App</h2>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Settings</h3>
<p class="doc-note">
    One form per section. A single form across the whole screen makes "Save" mean "save
    everything, including what I did not look at".
</p>
<Example Source="SettingsBlock.razor"><SettingsBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Navbar</h3>
<p class="doc-note">Links, a GET search, and an account menu on one bar. The phone menu is a <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, not state — it works with scripting off and never desyncs.</p>
<Example Source="NavbarBlock.razor"><NavbarBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">App shell</h3>
<p class="doc-note">The shadcn dashboard frame: collapsible sidebar, trigger plus breadcrumb header, content well. Collapse is a server-read cookie, so the first paint already matches.</p>
<Example Source="AppShellBlock.razor"><AppShellBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Onboarding</h3>
<p class="doc-note">Three steps, three URLs — Back works, refresh keeps your place, and a half-finished signup resumes from a link in an email.</p>
<Example Source="OnboardingBlock.razor"><OnboardingBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Profile</h3>
<p class="doc-note">Follow posts to itself and swaps only the button; Message goes to a conversation route, because a conversation is a URL worth sharing.</p>
<Example Source="ProfileBlock.razor"><ProfileBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Billing</h3>
<p class="doc-note">Money is formatted by the server. Plan changes are one POST per tier; destructive actions sit behind an AlertDialog, never beside Save.</p>
<Example Source="BillingBlock.razor"><BillingBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Timeline</h3>
<p class="doc-note">Each entry is an <code>&lt;article&gt;</code> with a real <code>&lt;time&gt;</code>; filtering is a GET form, so a filtered timeline is a shareable URL.</p>
<Example Source="TimelineBlock.razor"><TimelineBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Form wizard</h3>
<p class="doc-note">Each step is a real URL and a real POST, not a client-side index — which is what makes the back button work, a refresh safe, and a half-finished application resumable from a link.</p>
<Example Source="FormWizardBlock.razor"><FormWizardBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Master and detail</h3>
<p class="doc-note">The shape behind mail, chat and any inbox. Only the detail pane is swapped, so the list keeps its scroll and the address bar always names what is on screen.</p>
<Example Source="MasterDetailBlock.razor"><MasterDetailBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Notifications</h3>
<Example Source="NotificationsBlock.razor"><NotificationsBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Board</h3>
<p class="doc-note">
    No drag-and-drop, deliberately. Dragging has no keyboard equivalent unless you build one, and
    the accessible fallback everyone eventually bolts on is exactly this: a control on the card
    saying which column it belongs in. So the fallback is the interface.
</p>
<Example Source="KanbanBlock.razor"><KanbanBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Task tracker</h3>
<p class="doc-note">
    Ticking a box is a POST targeting <code>:origin</code>, so a list of two hundred tasks costs
    one row per tick and nothing below you moves.
</p>
<Example Source="TaskTrackerBlock.razor"><TaskTrackerBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">File manager</h3>
<p class="doc-note">
    Selection is real checkboxes sharing one name, and the bulk bar appears through
    <code>:has(:checked)</code> — CSS reading the boxes rather than a second copy of the selection
    that can drift from them.
</p>
<Example Source="FileManagerBlock.razor"><FileManagerBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Calendar</h3>
<p class="doc-note">
    Also a table, for the same reason. Month navigation is two links to real URLs, so a month is
    bookmarkable and Back moves through months. For picking a single date use
    <code>DatePicker</code> instead — this one is for showing what is on.
</p>
<Example Source="CalendarBlock.razor"><CalendarBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Chat</h3>
<p class="doc-note">
    The composer targets <code>.chat-thread:after</code> — append, not replace — so sending adds
    one bubble instead of re-rendering the conversation and losing your scroll. The thread polls
    with an <code>[up-etag]</code>, so a poll that finds nothing costs an empty 304.
</p>
<Example Source="ChatBlock.razor"><ChatBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Social feed</h3>
<p class="doc-note">
    Each post is an <code>&lt;article&gt;</code> with a real permalink. Optimistic UI is left out
    on purpose: the round trip is the truth here, and a counter that goes up and then back down is
    worse than one that waits.
</p>
<Example Source="SocialFeedBlock.razor"><SocialFeedBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Media player</h3>
<p class="doc-note">
    Built on <code>&lt;audio controls&gt;</code>, which already has seeking, volume, media keys and
    a lock-screen control on a phone. Hand-rolled scrubbers are where audio players lose their
    accessibility.
</p>
<Example Source="MediaPlayerBlock.razor"><MediaPlayerBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Mobile shell</h3>
<p class="doc-note">
    Three things make this feel like an app rather than a page, and all three are easy to leave
    out: <code>100dvh</code> rather than <code>100vh</code>, because a phone's browser chrome
    slides away and <code>vh</code> is frozen at its largest;
    <code>env(safe-area-inset-bottom)</code>, or the last tab sits under the home indicator and
    cannot be tapped; and a tab bar of real links with <code>[up-nav]</code>, so a tab is a URL
    that can be shared and the back button works.
</p>
<Example Source="MobileShellBlock.razor"><MobileShellBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Changelog</h3>
<p class="doc-note">Versions down the left with anchors, typed change chips down the right. Newest first — a changelog nobody can link into is a blog.</p>
<Example Source="ChangelogBlock.razor"><ChangelogBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Empty state</h3>
<Example Source="EmptyStateBlock.razor"><EmptyStateBlock /></Example>

<h3 class="mt-8 text-base font-semibold tracking-tight">Not found</h3>
<p class="doc-note">A 404 that gets visitors back to work: two actions (Home, and Back as a link — never a button that resubmits), plus the three pages mistyped URLs always land on.</p>
<Example Source="NotFoundBlock.razor"><NotFoundBlock /></Example>
